    {
      "title": "Module 1 \u00b7 Trig Ratios",
      "html": "<p>In a right-angled triangle with an acute angle $\\theta$:</p>$$\\sin\\theta=\\dfrac{\\text{opp}}{\\text{hyp}},\\quad \\cos\\theta=\\dfrac{\\text{adj}}{\\text{hyp}},\\quad \\tan\\theta=\\dfrac{\\text{opp}}{\\text{adj}}$$<p><b>Special angles</b>: $\\sin 30\u00b0 = \\tfrac{1}{2}$, $\\cos 30\u00b0 = \\tfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$, $\\tan 45\u00b0 = 1$, $\\sin 60\u00b0 = \\tfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>$\\sin 30\u00b0 = 1/2$.</li><li>$\\cos 60\u00b0 = 1/2$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate $\\sin 30\u00b0$.<br/><b>A:</b> \\dfrac{1}{2}</p><p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate $\\cos 60\u00b0$.<br/><b>A:</b> \\dfrac{1}{2}</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Find $\\tan 60\u00b0$ exactly.",
          "a": "$\\tan 60\u00b0 = \\sqrt 3$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Module 2 \u00b7 Elevation & Depression",
      "html": "<p>The <b>angle of elevation</b> is measured upward from the horizontal to the line of sight of a higher object. The <b>angle of depression</b> is measured downward from horizontal to a lower object. Both use the same value between the horizontal and the sight line.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>$\\tan 60\u00b0 = h/d \\Rightarrow h = 35\\tan 60\u00b0 \\approx 60.62$ m.</li><li>$\\tan 60\u00b0 = h/d \\Rightarrow h = 33\\tan 60\u00b0 \\approx 57.16$ m.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> A point is $35$ m from the base of a tower. The angle of elevation to the top is $60\u00b0$. Find the height of the tower.<br/><b>A:</b> 60.62 m</p><p><b>Q:</b> A point is $33$ m from the base of a tower. The angle of elevation to the top is $60\u00b0$. Find the height of the tower.<br/><b>A:</b> 57.16 m</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "From a point 20 m from a tower, the angle of elevation to the top is $30\u00b0$. Find the height of the tower.",
          "a": "$h = 20\\tan 30\u00b0 = 20/\\sqrt 3 \\approx 11.55\\ \\text{m}$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Module 3 \u00b7 Bearings",
      "html": "<p><b>Three-figure bearings</b> are measured clockwise from North (always 3 digits: $045\u00b0$, $270\u00b0$).</p><p><b>Compass bearings</b> use directions like N40\u00b0E (start from N or S, rotate through the given angle towards E or W).</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>$\\sin 30\u00b0 = 1/2$.</li><li>$\\cos 60\u00b0 = 1/2$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate $\\sin 30\u00b0$.<br/><b>A:</b> \\dfrac{1}{2}</p><p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate $\\cos 60\u00b0$.<br/><b>A:</b> \\dfrac{1}{2}</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "A boat sails N60\u00b0E for 100 km. What is its bearing in three-figure notation?",
          "a": "N60\u00b0E = $060\u00b0$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Trig ratios of acute and special angles",
      "html": "<p><b>Trig ratios of acute and special angles</b> is a key part of Trigonometry (SSS 2, Mathematics). Master the meaning, the main points and how it is tested in JAMB and WAEC questions on this topic.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>In a right-angled triangle with an acute angle $\\theta$: $$\\sin\\theta=\\dfrac{\\text{opp}}{\\text{hyp}},\\quad \\cos\\theta=\\dfrac{\\text{adj}}{\\text{hyp}},\\quad \\tan\\theta=\\dfrac{\\text{opp}}{\\text{adj}}$$ Special angles : $\\sin 30\u00b0 = \\tfrac{1}{2}$, $\\cos 30\u00b0 = \\tfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$, $\\tan 45\u00b0 = 1$, $\\sin 60\u00b0 = \\tfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate $\\sin 30\u00b0$.<br/><b>A:</b> \\dfrac{1}{2} \u2014 $\\sin 30\u00b0 = 1/2$.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate $\\cos 60\u00b0$.<br/><b>A:</b> \\dfrac{1}{2} \u2014 $\\cos 60\u00b0 = 1/2$.</p><h4>More key points</h4><ul><li>If $$ \\sin \\theta = 3/5 $$ and $$ \\theta $$ is acute, find $$ \\tan \\theta $$. \u2014 3/4.</li><li>If $$ \\tan \\theta = 1 $$, find $$ \\theta $$ (acute). \u2014 $$ 45^\\circ $$.</li><li>If $\\\\tan \\\\theta = \\\\frac{4}{3}$ and $\\\\theta$ is acute, find $\\\\sin \\\\theta + \\\\cos \\\\theta$. \u2014 $\\\\frac{7}{5}$.</li><li>If $\\\\sin \\\\theta = 0.6$, find $\\\\cos \\\\theta$ (for acute $\\\\theta$). \u2014 0.8.</li></ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Evaluate $\\sin 30\u00b0$.",
          "a": "\\dfrac{1}{2} \u2014 $\\sin 30\u00b0 = 1/2$."
        },
        {
          "q": "Evaluate $\\cos 60\u00b0$.",
          "a": "\\dfrac{1}{2} \u2014 $\\cos 60\u00b0 = 1/2$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Revision and exam focus",
      "html": "<p>Revise the whole topic by working through its key points in order, then test yourself with the 50-question quiz for this topic. Pay attention to definitions, differences between similar terms, and questions that ask you to apply the topic rather than only recall it.</p><h4>Worked examples</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate $\\sin 30\u00b0$.<br/><b>A:</b> \\dfrac{1}{2} \u2014 $\\sin 30\u00b0 = 1/2$.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate $\\cos 60\u00b0$.<br/><b>A:</b> \\dfrac{1}{2} \u2014 $\\cos 60\u00b0 = 1/2$.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate $\\tan 45\u00b0$.<br/><b>A:</b> 1 \u2014 $\\tan 45\u00b0 = 1$.</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Evaluate $\\sin 30\u00b0$.",
          "a": "\\dfrac{1}{2}"
        },
        {
          "q": "Evaluate $\\cos 60\u00b0$.",
          "a": "\\dfrac{1}{2}"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
